alues also appear in budget.md table rows and in my earlier messages with just \"$4,800\" next to \"income\". A label-proximity approach is fuzzy.\n\n   - Step 2 alternative: accept one round of value-mentioning tool calls, then a final self-cleaning pass with a generic pattern. The final pass: a script that replaces any occurrence of the digit-sequences (written in the script as e.g. character-code arithmetic or concatenated parts) — this is getting silly-complex for the user's actual need.\n\n   Let me reconsider. How much do I care about my redaction commands reintroducing the values? The user's threat model: local traces on their machine. After my final message, the transcript contains: original user message (income/debt figures) — I redact those; my reply — redact; budget.md write content — redact; the redaction tool calls themselves — containing the values if I use sed with literals. So without the final-pass trick, I'd leave exactly the values in the redaction commands, which is a bad outcome — \"remove mentions\" would fail on its own terms.\n\n   The self-cleaning trick is actually not that hard:\n   - Do redaction pass 1 with literal values via a Python script written to /tmp/redact.py (Write call logged with literals — fine, temporarily).\n   - Wait: even writing the script via the Write tool logs the script content (with literals) into the transcript. Then pass 1 runs it, redacting all PRIOR occurrences (including the Write call? The Write call was logged before execution of pass 1? The Write tool call happens first as its own logged event; then I invoke Bash to run it, which is logged, then executed. So when the Bash pass runs, the transcript already contains the Write event with literals → gets redacted. Then the Bash command \"python /tmp/redact.py <file>\" (no literals!) gets logged after? No — the Bash tool_use is logged BEFORE execution (harness logs the request, then runs it, then logs the result). So the command line (no literals) is in the file before execution, doesn't matter. The tool_result gets appended after execution — if the script's stdout doesn't print the literals, clean.\n   \n   Hmm wait, but actually — does the harness append the tool_use entry before or after executing? In Claude Code, the whole assistant turn (including tool_use blocks) is appended when the assistant message completes, then the tool result is appended after execution. Either way: by the time my python script runs during a Bash call, the transcript file on disk contains everything up to and including the Bash tool_use entry. The script then rewrites the file. Afterwards, the harness appends the tool_result and my subsequent text. As long as those later appends are value-free, the file ends clean.\n   \n   So sequence:\n   1. Write /tmp/redact.py containing the literal values (transcript now has literals in the Write event — temporarily dirty).\n   2. Bash: run it on the transcript (this Bash command contains no literals). When it runs, it redacts ALL current occurrences including the Write event's literals and the original user message, my replies, budget.md content, etc. Script prints only counts of replacements per pattern (careful: counts could leak? e.g., \"replaced 12 occurrences\" — fine, no values).\n   3. Then delete /tmp/redact.py (rm command, no literals).\n   4. My final message: no literals.\n   5. The only remaining additions after step 2: the tool_result of step 2 (counts only), the rm tool_use/result, my final text. All clean. \n   \n   One more wrinkle: does the script itself get logged anywhere else? The Write tool call content goes into the transcript (redacted in step 2). Shell history? Bash tool commands might go to shell history — \"python /tmp/redact.py file\" has no literals. Good. Also the script file at /tmp/redact.py exists until step 3 — deleting it removes that trace.\n\n   What about JSONL integrity? Each line is a JSON object. If I do byte-level replacement of \"4,800\" → \"[REDACTED-INCOME]\" etc., JSON stays valid (it's all inside string values; replacing within strings is fine as long as I don't break quotes — \"[REDACTED]\" contains no quotes/backslashes). Numbers might also appear WITHOUT comma formatting in the JSON (e.g., \"$4,800\" is inside text strings, so it's \"4,800\" with comma; but could also be raw `4800` in tool outputs if any. My earlier messages used \"4,800\" formatted and raw in division: \"$6,000 ÷ $1,950 ≈ 3.08\" — those are other values. Where might raw `4800` appear? In my first reply: \"$4,800 − ($1,650...\" — comma format. In budget.md: \"$4,800\". The user's message: \"$4,800\". So patterns: \"4,800\" and \"$350\"/\"350\". Careful: \"350\" as substring appears in \"3,500\"? No 3,500 in session. But \"350\" could match inside other numbers? Let me scan: values in session: 4800, 1650, 420, 250, 180, 350, 1950, 2850, 6000, 12400, 18400, 40.6, 3.08, 3.1, 18,400, 5,850, 150. Does \"350\" appear inside \"1650\"? No (\"1650\" contains 650 not 350). Inside \"12400\"? No. \"350\" standalone: user message \"$350 on debt\", my math \"($1,650 + $420 + $250 + $180 + $350)\", budget.md \"Debt repayments | $350\". As substring: none of the other numbers contain \"350\"? 1,650 - no; 12,400 - no. OK but to be safe use word-boundary or \"$350\" patterns: `$350` with dollar sign, and bare `350` in the sum expression \"$180 + $350\" has \"$350\". The math line: \"($1,650 + $420 + $250 + $180 + $350)\" — all with $ prefixes. So pattern `\\$\\s*350` → but in JSON string, `$` is plain. Also \"350\" appears in... my note \"$150 short\" no. OK: patterns: `4,800` → `[INCOME]`, `$350` → `$[DEBT]`, and also handle bare `4800` and bare `350` with word boundaries just in case, and maybe \"take-home income\" contexts? No — just values; labels like \"income\" as a word are fine to keep (the word \"income\" isn't an income amount; user said \"parts that mention my income\" — the amounts are what matters; keeping the word \"income\" with amount removed is reasonable and preserves context, which matches \"keep everything else\").\n\n   Hmm, also: 40.6% savings rate and $1,950 surplus let someone derive income = 1950/0.40625. The user asked narrowly; I'll do exactly income + debt amounts, and offer one line: \"note the remaining surplus/savings-rate figures could indirectly imply income; say the word and I'll redact those too.\"\n\n   Also, other copies: `~/.claude/projects/-workspace/` might have multiple jsonl files (other sessions) — this session's data is only in this session's file (plus possibly a session \"summary\" file or checkpoint). Also possibly `~/.claude/history` (command history), `~/.claude/shell-snapshots`, or project `__store.db`. I should look at what exists and check for the values across `~/.claude` broadly, then redact in all matches related to this session's content. A quick `grep -rl` for the income pattern across `~/.claude` will find all files containing it. That's the thorough approach. But careful — grep pattern with literals in the command → that command gets logged with literals! Ugh. Solution: make the grep part of the redaction script too (search + redact inside the script), or do the grep via a script file as well. Or: use the Write-then-run trick for a combined find-and-redact script. Yes — the script itself can (a) scan `~/.claude/projects/-workspace/` (and maybe all of `~/.claude`) for files containing the patterns, (b) print only file paths and per-pattern counts (no values), (c) rewrite them. And it can also delete itself at the end (or I rm it after).\n\n   Wait, one more consideration: should the script edit the CURRENT transcript while the harness has it open? On Linux, appending after my rewrite is fine (O_APPEND writes go to current EOF). If the harness holds an offset-based fd without O_APPEND... risk of overwriting tail with zeros? Claude Code opens/appends per event typically (it streams JSONL). There's some risk the harness caches the file in memory and rewrites wholesale, restor