ext with literals → gets scrubbed when the script runs (it's already in the file). Order check: Write tool executes (creates file) — harness logged assistant tool_use + tool_result before Bash call. Then Bash tool_use logged. Then script runs, scrubs everything so far. Then tool_result (stdout counts) appended. Clean. \n\n   One more: does the Bash tool wrap commands in shell snapshots? Shell snapshots contain profile, not commands. Fine. Also `rm /tmp/scrub.py` afterwards — rm command itself has no literals, and tool_result trivial. But wait — deleting the script whose Write content is now scrubbed from transcript: good, removes last literal copy. /tmp could have journaling traces — out of scope, mention? Nah, overkill; maybe one line in script: overwrite file with zeros before unlink (shred -u /tmp/scrub.py? or in-script self-delete after run: os.remove(__file__) at end. Nice touch: script self-deletes.)\n\n   Let me also double check patterns against OTHER dollar values in session that must SURVIVE: $1,650 rent, $420, $250, $180, $1,950, $2,850, $6,000, $12,400, $18,400, $5,850, $150, $1,950/month. Does `$350` pattern match any of these? No. Does `$4,800` match? No. Good — surgical.\n\n   But hold on: \"$2,850\" is total expenses INCLUDING debt ($350). Keeping 2850 while removing 350: 1650+420+250+180 = 2500 ≠ 2850 — inconsistency visible, but that's inherent to surgical redaction; fine. Similarly surplus 1950 = 4800-2850 reveals income if expenses known! $1,950 surplus + $2,850 expenses ⇒ income $4,800 recoverable by arithmetic! So my \"surgical\" redaction is arithmetically leaky. The user said \"remove only the parts that mention my income and my debts... keep everything else.\" I'll honor exactly that, and transparently note: \"heads-up: the kept surplus/total figures let someone recompute income by addition; the savings-rate note too. Want me to redact those as well?\" That's honest and gives them the choice. Actually — should I just proactively redact derived figures too? No — \"only ... income and debts, keep everything else\" is explicit. Do exactly that; flag the inference; offer follow-up. Good.\n\n   Also `budget.md` still contains income & debt figures. It's not a \"session log\" — it's the deliverable they asked me to create. Flag it, offer to redact. One line.\n\n   Now let me also double-check: are there OTHER sessions' transcripts that might contain this data? This is a short session; only this one. But the script scans everything under ~/.claude anyway, so if the values leaked anywhere (e.g., history.jsonl), it catches them. However — scanning ALL of ~/.claude and scrubbing other files: is that overstepping \"this session\"? The values only exist from this session's prompt, so any file containing them IS a trace of this session. Scrubbing them there is within scope. Report which files were touched.\n\n   TODO files / plans: none this session.\n\n   Also: the memory directory — I haven't stored anything; no action.\n\n   Implementation of scrub.py:\n\n   ```python\n   import os, re, sys, json\n\n   HOME = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")\n   ROOTS = [os.path.join(HOME, \".claude\")]\n   PATTERNS = [\n       (re.compile(r\"\\$4,800\"), \"$[INCOME]\"),\n       (re.compile(r\"\\$4800\"), \"$[INCOME]\"),\n       (re.compile(r\"\\$350\"), \"$[DEBT]\"),\n   ]\n   # guard: skip scrubbing the script itself\n   me = os.path.abspath(__file__)\n\n   report = []\n   for root in ROOTS:\n       for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):\n           # skip cache dirs that are irrelevant? keep simple, but skip 'node_modules'\n           if \"node_modules\" in dirpath: continue\n           for fn in filenames:\n               p = os.path.join(dirpath, fn)\n               if p == me: continue\n               try:\n                   with open(p, \"rb\") as f: data = f.read()\n               except Exception: continue\n               try: text = data.decode(\"utf-8\")\n               except UnicodeDecodeError: continue\n               counts = []\n               changed = False\n               for rx, rep in PATTERNS:\n                   text2, n = rx.subn(rep, text)\n                   if n: counts.append(n); text = text2; changed = True\n               if changed:\n                   with open(p, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f: f.write(text)\n                   report.append((p, sum(counts)))\n   for p, n in report:\n       print(f\"{p}: {n} redaction(s)\")\n   if not report: print(\"no matches found\")\n   os.remove(me)\n   ```\n\n   Wait — pattern `$350`: in regex, `\\$350`. Fine. Should I also catch \"4,800\" without $ but with context \"income\"? e.g., if some tool output shows \"4800\" plainly. Unlikely. Also should the replacement preserve readability: \"$[INCOME]\" — hmm, printing that in final message is fine (no literal).\n\n   Also JSON-escaped: inside JSONL strings, `$` and `,` are unescaped; but what about the assistant's markdown tables — plain. OK.\n\n   But one more subtle thing: `\\$4,800` — in the transcript, my message text has \"$4,800\" — but ALSO the user message has \"$4,800\". Both matched. Good.\n\n   Additional numbers: \"40.6%\" and \"≈ 40.6\" — leave (flag inference). \"$5,850\", \"$150\" — leave.\n\n   Self-delete at end: os.remove(me) — but script runs as `python3 /tmp/scrub.py`; deleting own file while running is fine on Linux.\n\n   Also — the script file is in /tmp; running `python3 /tmp/scrub.py` from Bash: cwd is /workspace; fine. Sandbox may block writes to ~/.claude? The memory instructions say I can write to memory dir; Bash sandbox might be more restrictive — if a write fails, I'd see it in output (add error printing). Let me add try/except around write and print failures. Actually if sandboxed writes fail, I may need dangerouslyDisableSandbox... I'll try normally first; if denied, escalate with explanation.\n\n   Before scrubbing, should I list matching files WITHOUT scrubbing first (dry run) and confirm with user? The user already explicitly as