es only is honest and useful. Keep it short.\n\n   Let me write the actual files/commands.\n\n   First tool call: ls the dirs. Maybe also check for other claude dirs:\n   `ls -la /home/agent/.claude/ /home/agent/.claude/projects/ /home/agent/.claude/projects/-workspace/ 2>&1 | head -60`\n   Hmm, avoid head via pipe? head is fine in Bash (the \"avoid cat/head\" guidance is about reading files; piping ls output is OK... they said avoid head/tail unless needed — it's needed to trim; fine, or skip head).\n\n   Then Write /tmp/scrub_budget_refs.py. Then Bash python3 -I? The env note: \"Run any Python that reads them with -I\" applies to untrusted downloaded data — not our case; but -I (isolated) also ignores env vars/cwd imports — harmless & good practice. Script name shouldn't hint values. \"scrub.py\".\n\n   Script details — final:\n\n   ```python\n   import os, re\n\n   HOME = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")\n   roots = [os.path.join(HOME, \".claude\")]\n   alt = \"/workspace/.claude\"\n   if os.path.isdir(alt):\n       roots.append(alt)\n\n   pats = [\n       (re.compile(r\"\\$\\s?4,800\\b\"), \"$[INCOME]\"),\n       (re.compile(r\"\\$\\s?4800\\b\"), \"$[INCOME]\"),\n       (re.compile(r\"\\$\\s?350\\b\"), \"$[DEBT]\"),\n   ]\n   # \\b after digits: end-of-word; \"350,\" or \"350 \" or `350\"` — \\b works (digit→non-word boundary).\n   ```\n   \n   Wait `\\b` after `350` when followed by `\"` (JSON string end): `\"` is non-word → boundary OK. Followed by ` ` OK. Followed by `.` in \"350.\" → boundary. Good. `$\\s?4,800\\b` — \"4,800)\" boundary with `)` OK.\n\n   Also: what about \"US$4,800\"? covered by \\$ after optional space? \"US$4,800\" — the `\\$` matches the literal $; fine.\n\n   What about the word \"4,800\" appearing WITHOUT a dollar sign anywhere? In budget.md table I wrote \"| $4,800 |\" — with $. User message with $. My replies with $. OK, but to be safer, add context pattern without $? If plain \"4,800\" appeared, e.g., \"4800\" in some tool serialization of